{"agent":"fleet-leaselapse","data":{"by":"k3","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"`own_lapsed` is defined as `existing is not None and existing.holder == holder and cfg.lease.resume_own` — it never checks that the lease actually lapsed. The knob's doc says \"The agent whose lease LAPSED resumes it\", and the very branch being bypassed distinguishes the two cases (`if existing.expired_at:` with an implied else for a live lease). So a plain `ddflow claim` of an item whose lease is still live and held by the same agent now skips the `reclaim_policy == \"report\"` handling entirely and falls through to the code path intended for fresh/resumed claims, instead of the existing-lease path. Given a live lease on T1 held by \"first\" and `resume_own` left at its default `true`, `LC.claim(repo, \"T1\", agent=\"first\")` bypasses the branch that previously handled \"you already hold this\", changing behavior for a case the feature never targeted. The condition should also require the lease to be expired (e.g. `existing.expired_at is not None` or equivalent) before short-circuiting.","digest":"5b6c546e6d5753c7","location":"ddflow/services/leases.py:294 (`own_lapsed`)","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/leases.py:294 (`own_lapsed`)"}],"chunks_total":1,"coverage":"1/1 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":4820,"diff_sha":"b44940857a59729c27079432d8b6474b5e8c585cf3c17bbacdcf2fafd03d761e","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":35.4,"family":"moonshot","findings":1,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"k3","output_digest":"2260d3a60742b923","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/DX-lease-lapse-reclaim.rubber_duck.kimi-k3.20261016T074522Z-9891c759.jsonl","reason":"","review_kind":"full","reviewed":[1],"reviewed_head":"f69bea9282c1ab5c09985972c2e99531793da5c4","reviewer":"kimi-k3","reviewer_digest":"afc8d76155f35170","round":1,"rounds":1,"stat